      "fact_text": {
        "text": "A primitive type does not have members, so using the dot operator on a primitive variable shows nothing.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Reference types are classes that have members (fields and methods) which can be accessed with the dot operator.",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Primitive types store simple values, while reference types store complex objects.",
        "refs": ["Q0003"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Variables of primitive types hold independent copies of values; changing one variable does not affect another variable that was assigned its value.",
        "refs": ["Q0003", "Q0004"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Variables of reference types hold references to objects; assigning one reference variable to another makes both refer to the same object, so changes via one reference affect the other.",
        "refs": ["Q0004"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    }
  ],
